WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.932
This is a research explainer from
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:04.932 --> 00:00:09.456
and it answers a question that comes up
in every Active Directory retirement:

4
00:00:09.456 --> 00:00:12.354
when AD goes, what happens
to all the certificates,

5
00:00:12.354 --> 00:00:14.687
tokens and keys it quietly looked after?

6
00:00:14.687 --> 00:00:19.729
We'll follow four paths: SQL
Server signing in with Entra ID,

7
00:00:19.729 --> 00:00:25.926
the clouds' own key vaults, the PIV card,
and the network's RADIUS and 802.1X.

8
00:00:25.926 --> 00:00:31.057
For each one we'll look at today, the
years of coexistence, and life after AD,

9
00:00:31.057 --> 00:00:36.275
and then put it all on one page: who
issues each credential, and who renews it.

10
00:00:36.275 --> 00:00:40.160
The slides and notes carry
the detail and the sources.

11
00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:45.010
Start with what AD actually does,
because most of it is invisible.

12
00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:49.747
It trusts the PIV card's issuer
and maps each card to an account.

13
00:00:49.747 --> 00:00:54.734
It hands out Kerberos tickets, so SQL
Server never has to think about tokens.

14
00:00:54.734 --> 00:00:58.685
It autoenrolls certificates for
domain controllers and servers.

15
00:00:58.685 --> 00:01:02.935
And through NPS, it answers the
network when a laptop plugs in.

16
00:01:02.935 --> 00:01:05.027
Each of those jobs needs a new owner,

17
00:01:05.027 --> 00:01:07.985
and the right-hand column
shows who takes each one.

18
00:01:07.985 --> 00:01:09.547
Some answers are obvious.

19
00:01:09.547 --> 00:01:12.373
Others, like RADIUS, surprise people.

20
00:01:12.373 --> 00:01:14.260
Let's go through them in order.

21
00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.935
Here's how the explainer is laid out.

22
00:01:16.935 --> 00:01:19.448
Every path is shown at three stages.

23
00:01:19.448 --> 00:01:21.747
Today, with AD at the center.

24
00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:24.435
Coexistence, which is the long middle,

25
00:01:24.435 --> 00:01:28.510
when AD and the cloud run side
by side and both have to work.

26
00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:32.035
And after AD, when the last
domain controller is gone.

27
00:01:32.035 --> 00:01:37.847
The four paths are SQL Server, the cloud
key vaults, PIV, and network access.

28
00:01:37.847 --> 00:01:39.997
The fifth part is the summary table.

29
00:01:39.997 --> 00:01:41.967
One rule runs through all of it:

30
00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:46.535
every credential has exactly one
renewer, and we write down who that is.

31
00:01:46.535 --> 00:01:48.690
First, SQL Server.

32
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:53.225
SQL Server can now accept
Entra ID tokens on premises,

33
00:01:53.225 --> 00:01:55.977
through Azure Arc, instead of Kerberos.

34
00:01:55.977 --> 00:01:58.391
That sounds like it removes certificates.

35
00:01:58.391 --> 00:01:59.277
It doesn't.

36
00:01:59.277 --> 00:02:02.609
Around one SQL Server there are
three different credentials,

37
00:02:02.609 --> 00:02:04.177
with three different owners.

38
00:02:04.177 --> 00:02:08.527
The TLS certificate, which CyberArk
Certificate Manager renews.

39
00:02:08.527 --> 00:02:14.514
On SQL Server 2022, an app-registration
certificate that lives in Key Vault.

40
00:02:14.514 --> 00:02:19.736
And on SQL Server 2025, a managed
identity that the Arc agent looks after,

41
00:02:19.736 --> 00:02:22.390
with no certificate to manage at all.

42
00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:25.151
The next slide explains
why the TLS certificate

43
00:02:25.151 --> 00:02:27.660
suddenly matters more than it used to.

44
00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:29.535
Here's the catch.

45
00:02:29.535 --> 00:02:33.522
Every connection authenticated
by Entra ID is encrypted.

46
00:02:33.522 --> 00:02:37.707
If the server only has its own
self-signed certificate, clients have to

47
00:02:37.707 --> 00:02:41.747
be told to trust it blindly, which
gives up checking who the server is.

48
00:02:41.747 --> 00:02:47.047
So the day Entra logins start, every instance
needs a real, CA-issued certificate.

49
00:02:47.047 --> 00:02:49.548
The CLM installs and renews it.

50
00:02:49.548 --> 00:02:52.119
There's one technical trap to test first:

51
00:02:52.119 --> 00:02:56.639
SQL Server only accepts a certificate
whose key was made the older way,

52
00:02:56.639 --> 00:03:01.236
and CyberArk's driver documentation
doesn't say which way it makes keys.

53
00:03:01.236 --> 00:03:02.435
Prove it in the lab.

54
00:03:02.435 --> 00:03:06.008
And on 2022, the app
certificate can rotate itself,

55
00:03:06.008 --> 00:03:08.810
if you set it up as service-managed.

56
00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.998
Put the stages side by side
and the direction is clear.

57
00:03:12.998 --> 00:03:16.972
Today, Windows authentication,
and TLS is optional.

58
00:03:16.972 --> 00:03:23.199
During coexistence, Windows and Entra
logins run together, TLS becomes mandatory,

59
00:03:23.199 --> 00:03:27.686
and SQL Server 2022 carries an
app certificate in Key Vault.

60
00:03:27.686 --> 00:03:32.313
After AD, it's Entra logins only,
applications use managed identities,

61
00:03:32.313 --> 00:03:37.185
and the TLS certificate comes from
the standalone CA under the same root.

62
00:03:37.185 --> 00:03:41.255
The program's advice is simple:
move to SQL Server 2025,

63
00:03:41.255 --> 00:03:44.123
so one whole certificate disappears.

64
00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:46.630
Next, the clouds' key vaults.

65
00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.099
Every cloud has its own
certificate service,

66
00:03:50.099 --> 00:03:53.480
and each one can issue and
renew certificates by itself.

67
00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:58.767
Azure has Key Vault, AWS has Certificate
Manager, and Google has its own.

68
00:03:58.767 --> 00:04:01.743
Nobody coordinates them
unless the program does.

69
00:04:01.743 --> 00:04:07.755
So the rule is: for each certificate, decide
one renewer, and record it in the CLM.

70
00:04:07.755 --> 00:04:11.424
Either the CLM issues it and
pushes it into the cloud,

71
00:04:11.424 --> 00:04:14.667
or the cloud issues it
and the CLM just watches.

72
00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:16.105
And notice the red band.

73
00:04:16.105 --> 00:04:18.305
Keys and secrets are a different job.

74
00:04:18.305 --> 00:04:21.830
They stay with each cloud's
own key and secret services.

75
00:04:21.830 --> 00:04:25.430
The CLM manages certificates, not secrets.

76
00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.380
The details differ a little by cloud.

77
00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.955
Key Vault only renews by itself for
its two partner CAs and for self-signed

78
00:04:32.955 --> 00:04:37.530
certificates, so a certificate from the
program's own CA comes from the CLM,

79
00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:42.330
and the good news is that the CLM's Key
Vault driver supports Azure Government.

80
00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:45.881
In AWS, Certificate Manager
renews what it issues,

81
00:04:45.881 --> 00:04:50.818
but never what you import, so imported
certificates belong to the CLM.

82
00:04:50.818 --> 00:04:52.117
Google is similar.

83
00:04:52.117 --> 00:04:55.353
One thing to confirm with
CyberArk in writing:

84
00:04:55.353 --> 00:04:58.418
its AWS driver pages
don't mention GovCloud.

85
00:04:58.418 --> 00:05:00.580
Now, the PIV card.

86
00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:04.455
The PIV card is the credential
that changes least.

87
00:05:04.455 --> 00:05:07.230
The card, the Federal
PKI issuer behind it,

88
00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:11.230
and the card management system
all stay exactly as they are.

89
00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:13.830
What changes is the system
that checks the card.

90
00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.441
Today that's AD: the domain controllers,

91
00:05:16.441 --> 00:05:21.580
the NTAuth store that trusts the issuer,
and the mapping from card to account.

92
00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:24.768
All of that, shown in red, ends with AD.

93
00:05:24.768 --> 00:05:29.268
On the right is what replaces it:
Entra certificate-based authentication,

94
00:05:29.268 --> 00:05:32.515
an attribute called
certificate user IDs that binds

95
00:05:32.515 --> 00:05:35.318
the card to the person,
and Conditional Access.

96
00:05:35.318 --> 00:05:37.720
Let's take the stages one at a time.

97
00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.108
Today, there's a deadline
that already passed.

98
00:05:41.108 --> 00:05:44.608
Since September 2025,
domain controllers refuse a

99
00:05:44.608 --> 00:05:48.358
certificate that isn't strongly
mapped to its account.

100
00:05:48.358 --> 00:05:53.201
Microsoft's easy fix, a security
identifier stamped inside the certificate,

101
00:05:53.201 --> 00:05:56.507
only works for certificates
from Microsoft's own CA.

102
00:05:56.507 --> 00:06:01.196
PIV certificates come from the Federal
PKI, so they need a different route.

103
00:06:01.196 --> 00:06:06.658
One is an explicit mapping on each account,
which IDManagement dot gov recommends.

104
00:06:06.658 --> 00:06:10.223
The other is a government-specific
policy Microsoft added,

105
00:06:10.223 --> 00:06:13.933
which trusts name-based mappings
from named federal issuers.

106
00:06:13.933 --> 00:06:15.570
The books now cover both.

107
00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:18.180
Next, the coexistence years.

108
00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:23.330
During coexistence, Entra ID
learns to check the card itself.

109
00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:26.352
You load the PIV issuers
into its trust store,

110
00:06:26.352 --> 00:06:29.537
give it a revocation list
it can reach over HTTP,

111
00:06:29.537 --> 00:06:34.355
and users can sign in to Windows with
their card on Entra-joined devices.

112
00:06:34.355 --> 00:06:38.550
The binding between card and person
lives in certificate user IDs,

113
00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.830
and for synced users only
Entra Connect can write it.

114
00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:46.614
There's one tension worth naming: the
federal guide suggests a looser binding,

115
00:06:46.614 --> 00:06:49.455
while Microsoft says use
only the strict kind.

116
00:06:49.455 --> 00:06:54.180
The program uses the strict kind,
tied to one specific certificate.

117
00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.570
So what happens when sync goes away?

118
00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:00.195
After AD, there's no sync
to carry the binding,

119
00:07:00.195 --> 00:07:02.432
so it has to be written in the cloud.

120
00:07:02.432 --> 00:07:06.202
The program's design is that the
card management system writes it,

121
00:07:06.202 --> 00:07:09.344
at the moment a card is issued,
through Microsoft Graph.

122
00:07:09.344 --> 00:07:11.709
That's the only moment
anyone knows the new

123
00:07:11.709 --> 00:07:14.545
certificate's serial number
before it's first used.

124
00:07:14.545 --> 00:07:19.220
HR can't do this job: HR knows
the person, not the certificate.

125
00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:21.775
Microsoft doesn't document this pattern,

126
00:07:21.775 --> 00:07:25.082
so it's design guidance, and
it needs proving in a pilot.

127
00:07:25.082 --> 00:07:28.857
Windows Hello stays the backup
for when a card isn't practical.

128
00:07:28.857 --> 00:07:32.350
Now, the one that
surprises people: RADIUS.

129
00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:35.233
When a laptop plugs into a switch,

130
00:07:35.233 --> 00:07:38.550
the switch asks a RADIUS
server whether to let it on.

131
00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:43.925
Today that's usually NPS, and NPS
checks with AD, so it ends with AD.

132
00:07:43.925 --> 00:07:48.350
People expect Microsoft to offer
a RADIUS service in Entra ID.

133
00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:49.237
It doesn't.

134
00:07:49.237 --> 00:07:50.776
So there are four choices.

135
00:07:50.776 --> 00:07:52.650
Keep NPS as a bridge.

136
00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:56.550
Move to a network access control
platform that checks Intune,

137
00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.462
which is the program's default.

138
00:07:58.462 --> 00:08:02.738
A cloud RADIUS service, except
none is FedRAMP-authorized yet.

139
00:08:02.738 --> 00:08:05.772
Or, for remote users,
Global Secure Access,

140
00:08:05.772 --> 00:08:10.770
which replaces the VPN, but not
the switch on the campus network.

141
00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.095
Here's how the default works.

142
00:08:13.095 --> 00:08:17.907
The device gets a certificate from
Intune with its Intune device ID inside.

143
00:08:17.907 --> 00:08:20.283
It presents that
certificate to the switch.

144
00:08:20.283 --> 00:08:23.914
The switch passes it to the
access control platform,

145
00:08:23.914 --> 00:08:28.982
Cisco ISE or Aruba ClearPass, which
asks Intune: is this device compliant?

146
00:08:28.982 --> 00:08:32.944
Then it tells the switch to let
it on, restrict it, or block it.

147
00:08:32.944 --> 00:08:34.207
Two things to watch.

148
00:08:34.207 --> 00:08:37.721
ISE needs a recent patch,
or these certificates fail.

149
00:08:37.721 --> 00:08:42.895
And the platform's own server certificate
isn't Intune's, so the CLM renews it.

150
00:08:42.895 --> 00:08:47.820
Today's NPS also hides a certificate
of its own that nobody tracked.

151
00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:52.170
Across the stages, the
network story is a handover.

152
00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:56.608
Today, NPS against AD, plus
a VPN that relies on it.

153
00:08:56.608 --> 00:09:00.963
During coexistence, Intune
devices move to the access control

154
00:09:00.963 --> 00:09:04.983
platform while older hybrid
devices still go through NPS.

155
00:09:04.983 --> 00:09:08.461
After AD, the platform
handles the campus network,

156
00:09:08.461 --> 00:09:13.044
and Global Secure Access or a
federated VPN handles remote users.

157
00:09:13.044 --> 00:09:16.031
Device certificates come
from Intune throughout,

158
00:09:16.031 --> 00:09:18.733
and the server certificates
come from the CLM.

159
00:09:18.733 --> 00:09:21.800
Now let's put all four paths on one page.

160
00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:24.062
This is the page to keep.

161
00:09:24.062 --> 00:09:27.158
Each row is a credential,
each column a stage,

162
00:09:27.158 --> 00:09:30.599
and each cell says who
issues it and who renews it.

163
00:09:30.599 --> 00:09:32.250
Read it for three patterns.

164
00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:34.737
The PIV row barely changes.

165
00:09:34.737 --> 00:09:38.213
The domain controller
and NPS rows simply end.

166
00:09:38.213 --> 00:09:41.891
And the CLM's rows stay steady
while the CA behind them

167
00:09:41.891 --> 00:09:45.401
moves from the retained AD
CS to the standalone one.

168
00:09:45.401 --> 00:09:48.763
Wherever two systems could
renew the same certificate,

169
00:09:48.763 --> 00:09:51.530
the program picks one, and writes it down.

170
00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:55.982
Finally, this research changed
the books in five places:

171
00:09:55.982 --> 00:10:01.534
the SQL Server and application chapters
on how certificates rotate and who renews

172
00:10:01.534 --> 00:10:05.477
them, Book 07 on federal smart
card mapping, and Book 09 on the

173
00:10:05.477 --> 00:10:10.305
forgotten NPS certificate and the
access control server's certificate.

174
00:10:10.305 --> 00:10:14.222
The next steps are tests, not more
reading: prove the SQL key type,

175
00:10:14.222 --> 00:10:19.268
prove the card system can write the binding,
and get CyberArk's answer on GovCloud.

176
00:10:19.268 --> 00:10:22.159
This is the program's
research, under review,

177
00:10:22.159 --> 00:10:25.130
and not an endorsement
by any federal agency.

178
00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:26.290
Thanks for watching.
